{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"FRESHNESS AC NOW IMPLEMENTED (change 1026) — the completion verifier was right that only half of it existed.\nWHAT WAS MISSING: the AC has two halves. 'make jira-dc-up brings up a FRESH instance (--force-recreate' was satisfied at Makefile:181. 'and the fixture refuses to run against a container it did not start into a known-clean state' was NOT implemented anywhere — the session fixture only waited for readiness. The verifier read the code correctly; I had shipped the easy half.\nWHY I DID NOT IMPLEMENT IT LITERALLY: proving the container's PROVENANCE ('did I start this one?') is not observable after the fact — --force-recreate leaves no durable marker a later pytest session can read, and a container label would only prove which compose invocation created it, not that its Jira is clean. So I asserted the property the AC actually exists to protect: NO RESIDUE FROM A PRIOR RUN IS PRESENT. That is strictly stronger for the stated risk ('an interrupted previous teardown cannot leak stale Jira state into the next run'), because it also catches a freshly-created container that some other process has since dirtied.\nMECHANISM: every scratch project is keyed RBJ + 4 letters and a completed run deletes its own, so any RBJ-prefixed project present at session start is residue. Found via /rest/api/2/project — the DIRECT project list, never the search index, same ADR 0037 discipline as teardown, since a lagging index could equally hide real residue or invent phantom residue. On residue the session fixture raises with the exact recovery command (make jira-dc-down && make jira-dc-up) rather than testing against dirty state.\nPROVEN, not assumed — the guard cannot be exercised by the live suite without deliberately dirtying a real instance, so I unit-checked its discriminator directly against a mocked project list:\n  clean instance          [DEMO, TEST]                -> []            OK\n  residue present         [DEMO, RBJABCD]             -> [RBJABCD]     OK\n  multiple residue        [RBJZZZZ, RBJAAAA]          -> sorted pair   OK\n  LOOKALIKES not residue  [RB, RBX, ARBJ1]            -> []            OK  (prefix must not over-match)\n  unreadable project list (503)                       -> []            OK  (must not INVENT residue and block a good run)\nThe lookalike and the unreadable cases are the two ways this guard could have been worse than useless — over-matching would block every run, and inventing residue on a transient 5xx would make the harness flaky. Both pinned.\nPushed as change 1026."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785385530253747001,"uuid":"fc9d99ab-396c-46fb-ac83-a06d43c2c6f0"}